\entrytitle{85}{Claw finding between two functions}{strengthPolynomial}{evidenceProved}{hardwareExponential}
\setcounter{problem}{84}
\begin{problem}[Claw finding between two functions]
Given evaluation oracles for $f:[N]\to Z$ and $g:[M]\to Z$, with $N\le M$, find $(x,y)$ with $f(x)=g(y)$ or determine that none exists. Claws arise when matching two halves of a cryptanalytic computation, including double encryption and prescribed-degree isogeny search. Complexity is measured in evaluation-oracle queries; runtime includes list-access costs, and explicit functions are additionally compared in gate and bit complexity.
\end{problem}

\speedupheading
\complexity{Quantum}{$O((NM)^{1/3})$ queries for $N\le M<N^2$, and $O(\sqrt M)$ for $M\ge N^2$, Tani quantum-walk claw finding.}
The balanced case is $\Theta(N^{2/3})$. The algorithm stores evaluated subsets of both domains; coherent sorting or lookup contributes to its gate cost. The stated bounded-error bounds include finding a claw or deciding that none exists, for the evaluation-oracle model. Quantum-walk spectral and marked-fraction parameters determine the two regimes.

\complexity{Classical}{$\Theta(N+M)$ queries in the worst case, classical list matching.}
Hashing both lists gives expected linear-time matching with suitable value representation; sorting gives a logarithmic time overhead. For $N=M$, the bound is $\Theta(N)$. If $f$ and $g$ have explicit verification circuits of combined size $G$ and the combined witness length is $\ell=\lceil\log_2N\rceil+\lceil\log_2M\rceil$, circuit encoding gives $\operatorname{poly}(\ell+G)2^{O((\ell+G)/\sqrt{\log(\ell+G)})}$ classical search. In the balanced case with $G=O(\log N)$, this becomes $N^{o(1)}$.

\dimensionlabel{Strength}{strengthPolynomial} Polynomial speedup, with degree $3/2$ in the balanced query comparison and quadratic search in the highly unbalanced regime. Quantum walks provide the balanced improvement.

\dimensionlabel{Evidence}{evidenceProved} Fully proved for the evaluation-oracle task and its matching query lower bounds. Explicit small circuits have the separate representation-dependent classical comparator.

\dimensionlabel{Hardware}{hardwareExponential} Time-efficient walks need coherent access to stored function values and exponential storage in the logarithmic domain size. Reversible function evaluation and walk updates require fault tolerance for cryptographic domains.

{\small [Before ``ETH is false'', the balanced generic comparison was $\Theta(N)$ classical versus $\Theta(N^{2/3})$ quantum queries, also transferred to explicit function evaluations.]\par}
\subhead{Algorithm}
\begin{algorithmindex}{85}
\item \href{https://arxiv.org/pdf/0708.2584}{Tani, \emph{Claw Finding Algorithms Using Quantum Walk} (2009)}. Section 3: claw detection; Section 4, \textsc{ClawSearch} and Theorem 8: finding a claw and the two domain-size regimes.
\item \href{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf}{OpenAI, \emph{The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False} (2026)}. Theorem 1.1 and witness self-reduction: search for a satisfying assignment of the explicit Boolean verification circuit.
\end{algorithmindex}
